ds can fit in a healthy pattern, but sometimes foods are eaten less often and in smaller amounts.","Name a specific source, cue or example and say what it shows about everyday foods.","Make a bigger claim without naming any source or cue.","Copy a classmate’s sentence with no local detail."],"question":"Which habit strengthens evidence about sometimes foods?","type":"multiple_choice"},{"correct_index":2,"explanation":"The stronger choice matches the taught idea with concrete detail.","options":["Treat a skipped permission or safety step as if it did not matter.","Ignore the safety rule because the first idea looked exciting.","Example. Everyday: wholegrain crackers with cheese, carrot sticks, a banana, water. Sometimes: a birthday cupcake, a packet of chips. Tia…","Believing sometimes foods are never allowed. All foods can fit in a healthy pattern, but sometimes foods are eaten less often and in smaller amounts."],"question":"Using this lesson idea — Everyday foods come from the five food groups and give the body the nutrients it needs for growth and… — which choice is best?","type":"multiple_choice"},{"correct_index":0,"explanation":"Respect and accuracy mean checking evidence and honouring limits/protocols.","options":["Check facts/cues, acknowledge limits, and avoid inventing details.","Invent missing details to make the answer sound complete.","Ignore safety or cultural protocols to finish faster.","Treat every online claim as equally reliable."],"question":"Which action shows respect and accuracy together?","type":"multiple_choice"},{"correct_index":1,"explanation":"Independence requires a new example that still uses the skill.","options":["Believing sometimes foods are never allowed. All foods can fit in a healthy pattern, but sometimes foods are eaten less often and in smaller amounts.","Make a two-column chart with five everyday and five sometimes foods that your family eats. Write the reason for each choice (nutrients, sugar, salt…","Retype the worked example with no new example.","Copy the example and call that independent work."],"question":"Which task best shows independent use of everyday foods?","type":"multiple_choice"},{"explanation":"Independent evidence on new material demonstrates the skill; copying the model does not.","marking_guide":"Require independently produced work with a specific supporting detail.","question":"Produce a fresh example demonstrating everyday foods on new material (not the worked model). Explain the particular words, sources or choices that make it work.","sample_answer":"Original response on new material: Everyday: apple slices, plain popcorn, milk. Sometimes: lollies and sausage roll (pastry and processed meat have a lot of saturated fat and salt). Plain popcorn without added butter or sugar is a… Reasoning: I used everyday foods by attending to sometimes foods rather than copying the worked model.","type":"short_answer"},{"explanation":"A useful correction changes the inaccurate idea or method and explains why.","marking_guide":"Require misconception + correction + purpose-linked reason.","question":"Name one likely error with everyday foods, correct it, and explain why the correction helps a reader or audience.","sample_answer":"Common error: Believing sometimes foods are never allowed. All foods can fit in a healthy pattern, but sometimes foods are eaten less often and in smaller amounts. Correction: Everyday: apple slices, plain popcorn, milk. Sometimes: lollies and sausage roll (pastry and processed meat have a lot of saturated fat and salt). Plain… Why: the correction restores accurate use of everyday foods.","type":"short_answer"}],"resources":null,"response_prompt":"Include your independent evidence and reflection, with reasons where requested. Share only what you feel comfortable sharing.","review_status":"unreviewed","seed_key":"s2-pdhpe-y3-w13-l1","sort_activity":null,"source_note":"Proposed Stage 2 NESA PDHPE alignment using catalogue outcome codes (PH2-*); content-point mapping remains pending human review. Physical activities are optional, adult-supervised and adaptable; no child is required to share personal details.","spelling":null,"spelling_focus":null,"stage":"S2","stage_hints":{"accept":["Today you will practise everyday foods.","Read the mission before you start moving or deciding.","Keep your own safety and everyone''s inclusion in view."],"guided":["Attempt the guided prompt without looking ahead.","Afterwards, compare your reasons with the feedback.","Adjust anything that does not keep everyone safe and included."],"learn":["Study how the first demonstration is built.","Point to the cue or rule that carries the idea.","Then write the reason without copying every sentence."],"quiz":["Read every option before you choose.","Eliminate answers that ignore safety, consent or inclusion.","Prefer a reasoned, respectful choice over one that merely looks fun."],"submit":["Confirm that your reasons and reflection are both present.","Proofread names, steps and safety words.","Make sure the final evidence matches the lesson purpose."],"teach0":["Read the stage title and find the worked example.","Name one exact cue, rule or choice before you decide.","Think through the small prompt before opening the comparison."],"teach1":["Return to the example and list the key cues.","Say what each cue does for safety, health or friendship.","A final label alone is weaker than a stated reason."],"teach2":["Compare an unsafe or unkind choice with a better one.","Change the choice or strategy, not only the words.","Name the mistake you are repairing."],"teach3":["Choose a fresh place, game or situation that still uses the same skill.","Write one reason in your own words.","Do not retype the worked example as your answer."],"write":["Follow every part of the independent instructions.","Include original reasons and a short reflection.","Save work that shows the skill in a new situation."]},"subject":"Healthy, Safe and Active Lifestyles","success_criteria":["I can use everyday foods with a specific example from this lesson.","I can identify sometimes foods and explain why it matters.","I can practise balance independently on a new prompt.","I can notice nutrients needs and correct a likely mistake."],"suspense_builder":null,"teach_steps":[{"check":{"correct_index":3,"explanation":"Everyday foods come from the five food groups and give the body the nutrients it needs for growth and energy. Sometimes foods, such as chips, lollies, soft drink and cake, are often high in added sugar, salt or saturated fat and low in nutrients, so they are eaten less often…","options":["A choice unrelated to safety, health or respect.","An answer copied without understanding.","A guess with no reason.","Everyday foods come from the five food groups and give the body the nutrients it needs for growth and energy. Sometimes foods, such as chips, lollies, soft drink and cake, are often high in added sugar, salt or saturated fat and low in nutrients, so they are eaten less often and in small amounts. Celebrations and treats are part of life; the aim is balance, not guilt."],"question":"Which choice best shows accurate use of this idea about everyday foods?","type":"multiple_choice"},"example":"WORKED MODEL\nExample. Everyday: wholegrain crackers with cheese, carrot sticks, a banana, water. Sometimes: a birthday cupcake, a packet of chips. Tia enjoys the cupcake at her party and chooses fruit and water at snack time, so her week stays balanced.\n\nTRY FIRST\nSort these foods into everyday or sometimes: apple slices, lollies, plain popcorn, sausage roll, milk. Give a reason for the sausage roll.\n\nFEEDBACK\nEveryday: apple slices, plain popcorn, milk. Sometimes: lollies and sausage roll (pastry and processed meat have a lot of saturated fat and salt). Plain popcorn without added butter or sugar is a grain food.","explain":"Everyday foods come from the five food groups and give the body the nutrients it needs for growth and energy. Sometimes foods, such as chips, lollies, soft drink and cake, are often high in added sugar, salt or saturated fat and low in nutrients, so they are eaten less often and in small amounts. Celebrations and treats are part of life; the aim is balance, not guilt.\n\nWHY IT MATTERS\nThis helps the learner make safe, healthy and respectful choices using clear cues and reasons, rather than guessing or copying others. Every choice keeps bodies safe and includes everyone.\n\nHOW TO WORK\nRead the worked example and name the cues, rules or choices that carry the idea. Before looking at the correction, make your own attempt using the exact question provided. For any movement, use a clear space, ask an adult to be nearby, move at your own comfortable level and stop if anything hurts or feels unsafe. You may always choose a gentler version or only describe the movement. One deliberate, reasoned change is more useful than a longer answer.","icon":"📖","notice":"Believing sometimes foods are never allowed. All foods can fit in a healthy pattern, but sometimes foods are eaten less often and in smaller amounts. Change the choice or strategy, not just the wording.","title":"Understand and demonstrate","visual":[{"alt":"Everyday and sometimes foods.","caption":"Balance over the week.","svg":"<svg xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/2000/svg\" viewBox=\"0 0 660 150\"><rect x=\"10\" y=\"12\" width=\"310\" height=\"126\" rx=\"14\" fill=\"#fff4ea\" stroke=\"#b87445\"/><rect x=\"340\" y=\"12\" width=\"310\" height=\"126\" rx=\"14\" fill=\"#e8f5ed\" stroke=\"#297a61\"/><g font-size=\"16\" fill=\"#263948\"><text x=\"28\" y=\"42\">Everyday</text><text x=\"28\" y=\"78\">fruit, vegetables, milk</text><text x=\"358\" y=\"42\">Sometimes</text><text x=\"358\" y=\"78\">lollies, chips, cake</text></g></svg>"}],"visual_before":[{"episode":{"scenes":[{"bg":"room","lines":[{"text":"I can see the example, but I am not sure which cue or rule matters most.","who":"pip"},{"text":"Name the cues, rules or choices that keep everyone safe and included before you decide.","who":"juno"},{"text":"I will try the prompt myself, then compare with the feedback.","who":"pip"},{"pause_after":true,"text":"Pause here. Use this example: Example. Everyday: wholegrain crackers with cheese, carrot sticks, a banana, water. Sometimes: a birthday cupcake, a pac Then write your own reasons.","who":"juno"}]}],"title":"Talk through the first model","transcript":"Pip: I can see the example, but I am not sure which cue or rule matters most.\nJuno: Name the cues, rules or choices that keep everyone safe and included before you decide.\nPip: I will try the prompt myself, then compare with the feedback.\nJuno: Pause here. Use this example: Example. Everyday: wholegrain crackers with cheese, carrot sticks, a banana, water. Sometimes: a birthday cupcake, a pac Then write your own reasons."}}]},{"check":{"correct_index":2,"explanation":"Make the PDHPE idea visible: name the body cue, safety rule or wellbeing strategy you used and say why it helps you or others. Supported choices follow the actual cues, rules and purpose of the task.","options":["An answer copied without understanding.","A guess with no reason.","Make the PDHPE idea visible: name the body cue, safety rule or wellbeing strategy you used and say why it helps you or others.","A choice unrelated to safety, health or respect."],"question":"Which choice best shows accurate use of this idea about everyday foods?","type":"multiple_choice"},"example":"WORKED MODEL\nExample. Everyday: wholegrain crackers with cheese, carrot sticks, a banana, water. Sometimes: a birthday cupcake, a packet of chips. Tia enjoys the cupcake at her party and chooses fruit and water at snack time, so her week stays balanced. Look at each cue, rule or choice that carries the idea.\n\nTRY FIRST\nList the key cues, rules or choices from the example and say what each one does for safety, health or relationships.\n\nFEEDBACK\nThe most useful reasoning names the cue, rule or strategy and the reason it helps, not only a label such as \"good\".","explain":"Make the PDHPE idea visible: name the body cue, safety rule or wellbeing strategy you used and say why it helps you or others.\n\nWHY IT MATTERS\nThis helps the learner make safe, healthy and respectful choices using clear cues and reasons, rather than guessing or copying others. Every choice keeps bodies safe and includes everyone.\n\nHOW TO WORK\nRead the worked example and name the cues, rules or choices that carry the idea. Before looking at the correction, make your own attempt using the exact question provided. For any movement, use a clear space, ask an adult to be nearby, move at your own comfortable level and stop if anything hurts or feels unsafe. You may always choose a gentler version or only describe the movement. One deliberate, reasoned change is more useful than a longer answer.","icon":"📖","notice":"Writing only a final label without a reason does not show PDHPE thinking. Change the choice or strategy, not just the wording.","title":"Show the reasoning","visual":[{"alt":"Balance across a day.","caption":"No food is bad.","svg":"<svg xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/2000/svg\" viewBox=\"0 0 660 150\"><rect x=\"10\" y=\"12\" width=\"310\" height=\"126\" rx=\"14\" fill=\"#fff4ea\" stroke=\"#b87445\"/><rect x=\"340\" y=\"12\" width=\"310\" height=\"126\" rx=\"14\" fill=\"#e8f5ed\" stroke=\"#297a61\"/><g font-size=\"16\" fill=\"#263948\"><text x=\"28\" y=\"42\">Party</text><text x=\"28\" y=\"78\">cupcake is fine</text><text x=\"358\" y=\"42\">Snack time</text><text x=\"358\" y=\"78\">fruit and water</text></g></svg>"}]},{"check":{"correct_index":1,"explanation":"Use an intentionally unsafe or unhelpful choice as a comparison. Name the specific PDHPE error, then show the safer or more respectful choice. Supported choices follow the actual cues, rules and purpose of the task.","options":["A guess with no reason.","Use an intentionally unsafe or unhelpful choice as a comparison. Name the specific PDHPE error, then show the safer or more respectful choice.","A choice unrelated to safety, health or respect.","An answer copied without understanding."],"question":"Which choice best shows accurate use of this idea about everyday foods?","type":"multiple_choice"},"example":"WORKED MODEL\nBelieving sometimes foods are never allowed. All foods can fit in a healthy pattern, but sometimes foods are eaten less often and in smaller amounts. Here is a more helpful example: Example. Everyday: wholegrain crackers with cheese, carrot sticks, a banana, water. Sometimes: a birthday cupcake, a packet of chips. Tia enjoys the cupcake at her party and chooses fruit and water at snack time, so her week stays balanced.\n\nTRY FIRST\nDescribe why the warned-against choice does not work, then write a safer, kinder or healthier choice with a reason.\n\nFEEDBACK\nEveryday: apple slices, plain popcorn, milk. Sometimes: lollies and sausage roll (pastry and processed meat have a lot of saturated fat and salt). Plain popcorn without added butter or sugar is a grain food.","explain":"Use an intentionally unsafe or unhelpful choice as a comparison. Name the specific PDHPE error, then show the safer or more respectful choice.\n\nWHY IT MATTERS\nThis helps the learner make safe, healthy and respectful choices using clear cues and reasons, rather than guessing or copying others. Every choice keeps bodies safe and includes everyone.\n\nHOW TO WORK\nRead the worked example and name the cues, rules or choices that carry the idea. Before looking at the correction, make your own attempt using the exact question provided. For any movement, use a clear space, ask an adult to be nearby, move at your own comfortable level and stop if anything hurts or feels unsafe. You may always choose a gentler version or only describe the movement. One deliberate, reasoned change is more useful than a longer answer.","icon":"📖","notice":"Changing the wording without changing the choice does not fix the error. Believing sometimes foods are never allowed. All foods can fit in a healthy pattern, but sometimes foods are eaten less often and in smaller amounts. Change the choice or strategy, not just the wording.","title":"Test a misconception"},{"check":{"correct_index":0,"explanation":"Use the same PDHPE idea in a new place, game or situation, not a copy of the example. Review safety, fairness and inclusion. Supported choices follow the actual cues, rules and purpose of the task.","options":["Use the same PDHPE idea in a new place, game or situation, not a copy of the example. Review safety, fairness and inclusion.","A choice unrelated to safety, health or respect.","An answer copied without understanding.","A guess with no reason."],"question":"Which choice best shows accurate use of this idea about everyday foods?","type":"multiple_choice"},"example":"WORKED MODEL\nEveryday foods come from the five food groups and give the body the nutrients it needs for growth and energy. Sometimes foods, such as chips, lollies, soft drink and cake, are often high in added sugar, salt or saturated fat and low in nutrients, so they are eaten less often and in small amounts. Celebrations and treats are part of life; the aim is balance, not guilt. Now choose a different situation to show you can transfer the idea.\n\nTRY FIRST\nDescribe or practise one fresh situation that uses the same skill and write a short reason for your choice.\n\nFEEDBACK\nThe new situation should use the same PDHPE idea as the example, but happen in a different place or with different people.","explain":"Use the same PDHPE idea in a new place, game or situation, not a copy of the example. Review safety, fairness and inclusion.\n\nWHY IT MATTERS\nThis helps the learner make safe, healthy and respectful choices using clear cues and reasons, rather than guessing or copying others. Every choice keeps bodies safe and includes everyone.\n\nHOW TO WORK\nRead the worked example and name the cues, rules or choices that carry the idea. Before looking at the correction, make your own attempt using the exact question provided. For any movement, use a clear space, ask an adult to be nearby, move at your own comfortable level and stop if anything hurts or feels unsafe. You may always choose a gentler version or only describe the movement. One deliberate, reasoned change is more useful than a longer answer.","icon":"📖","notice":"Repeating the worked example cannot show transfer to a new situation. Change the choice or strategy, not just the wording.","title":"Transfer to a new situation"}],"title":"Year 3, Week 13, Lesson 1: Everyday foods and sometimes foods","unit_number":3,"unit_title":"Healthy food and nutrition","week":13,"week_in_unit":3,"word_challenges":null,"worked_example":"WORK THROUGH THIS\nExample. Everyday: wholegrain crackers with cheese, carrot sticks, a banana, water. Sometimes: a birthday cupcake, a packet of chips. Tia enjoys the cupcake at her party and chooses fruit and water at snack time, so her week stays balanced.\n\nWrite what makes this example safe, healthy or respectful, naming the cues, rules or choices used.","worked_visuals":[{"alt":"Everyday and sometimes foods.","caption":"Balance over the week.","svg":"<svg xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/2000/svg\" viewBox=\"0 0 660 150\"><rect x=\"10\" y=\"12\" width=\"310\" height=\"126\" rx=\"14\" fill=\"#fff4ea\" stroke=\"#b87445\"/><rect x=\"340\" y=\"12\" width=\"310\" height=\"126\" rx=\"14\" fill=\"#e8f5ed\" stroke=\"#297a61\"/><g font-size=\"16\" fill=\"#263948\"><text x=\"28\" y=\"42\">Everyday</text><text x=\"28\" y=\"78\">fruit, vegetables, milk</text><text x=\"358\" y=\"42\">Sometimes</text><text x=\"358\" y=\"78\">lollies, chips, cake</text></g></svg>"},{"alt":"Balance across a day.","caption":"No food is bad.","svg":"<svg xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/2000/svg\" viewBox=\"0 0 660 150\"><rect x=\"10\" y=\"12\" width=\"310\" height=\"126\" rx=\"14\" fill=\"#fff4ea\" stroke=\"#b87445\"/><rect x=\"340\" y=\"12\" width=\"310\" hei